---
/**
 * The research journey.
 *
 * Every other surface presents the answer. This one presents the investigation, in the
 * order it actually happened, including the two places where the result was not what the
 * project expected.
 *
 * Written as a sequence of stages rather than a narrative essay, so a reader can enter at
 * whichever step they doubt. Each stage states what was expected, what was found, and what
 * changed as a result — and links to the surface where that step's evidence lives, so the
 * story is navigable rather than merely readable.
 *
 * The numbers are interpolated from the generated payloads, not written into the prose.
 * A story about a result is the easiest place for a stale figure to survive, because
 * nobody re-checks a narrative.
 *
 * Zero JavaScript.
 */
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import AmbientBackdrop from "@/components/shell/AmbientBackdrop.astro";
import PageHeader from "@/components/shell/PageHeader.astro";
import PageContract from "@/components/shell/PageContract.astro";
import Section from "@/components/shell/Section.astro";
import FlowDiagram from "@/components/evidence/FlowDiagram.astro";
import LearnMore from "@/components/editorial/LearnMore.astro";
import models from "@/generated/data/findings/models.json";
import benchmark from "@/generated/data/findings/benchmark.json";
import metrics from "@/generated/data/findings/metrics.json";
import card from "@/generated/data/dataset/card.json";

const nowcast = models.data.tasks[0]!;
const threshold = nowcast.models.find((m) => m.key === "threshold_rate")!;
const lgbm = nowcast.models.find((m) => m.key === "lightgbm")!;
const bench = benchmark.data;
const id = card.data.identity;

const TASK = "M/X NOWCAST";
const m = (metrics.tasks as Record<string, { calibration: { brier: number } }>)[TASK]!;

const fmt = (v: number | null | undefined, dp = 3): string =>
  typeof v === "number" && Number.isFinite(v) ? v.toFixed(dp) : "—";

const alarmRatio = lgbm.event.false_event_runs / threshold.event.false_event_runs;

const STAGES = [
  {
    n: "01",
    title: "Research question",
    expected: null,
    body:
      "Aditya-L1 publishes soft X-ray measurements from two payloads at one-minute cadence. " +
      "Can a model trained on that archive detect solar flares better than the simplest rule " +
      "an operator would write by hand?",
    found:
      "The question was fixed before the data was touched, along with the metrics that would " +
      "answer it. That ordering is what makes the eventual negative result publishable rather " +
      "than a story assembled after the fact.",
    href: "/overview/",
    hrefLabel: "What the project is",
  },
  {
    n: "02",
    title: "Initial hypothesis",
    expected: "Machine learning would beat the threshold, and spectral features would be why.",
    body:
      "The reasoning was ordinary and, at the time, sound. A single count-rate threshold uses " +
      "one number per minute and discards everything else — the shape of the rise, the recent " +
      "variance, the spectral distribution. A learner with fourteen features and access to the " +
      "spectrum should have had a great deal to work with.",
    found: "Neither half of the hypothesis survived contact with the evaluation.",
    href: "/models/",
    hrefLabel: "The models that were tried",
  },
  {
    n: "03",
    title: "Dataset",
    expected: null,
    body:
      `Two years of archive products were parsed onto a canonical one-minute grid: ` +
      `${id.n_parquet_files.toLocaleString("en-US")} Parquet files across seven tables, ` +
      `frozen at a single digest and never modified since. Missing minutes were left missing.`,
    found:
      "The limitations were written down before modelling, not after. Two of them decided the " +
      "shape of everything that followed: the effective sample size is events rather than " +
      "minutes, and no instrument response exists, so physical severity targets are " +
      "unconstructible from this archive.",
    href: "/data/card/#limitations",
    hrefLabel: "The ten limitation clauses",
  },
  {
    n: "04",
    title: "Baseline",
    expected: "A threshold would be a floor to clear.",
    body:
      `A single threshold on the SoLEXS count rate detected ` +
      `${threshold.event.events_detected} of ${threshold.event.n_events} M/X events ` +
      `(event recall ${fmt(threshold.event.event_recall)}) while raising ` +
      `${threshold.event.false_event_runs} false alarm runs.`,
    found:
      "The baseline was not a floor. It was, and remained, the best operational detector in " +
      "the study. Everything after this point is an attempt to displace it.",
    href: "/models/threshold_rate/",
    hrefLabel: "Threshold detector card",
  },
  {
    n: "05",
    title: "Machine learning",
    expected: "Gradient boosting would win.",
    body:
      `Logistic regression, a random forest and LightGBM were fitted on the same fourteen ` +
      `features under a protocol frozen in advance — seed ${models.data.seed}, chronological ` +
      `split, no shuffling. LightGBM ranked minutes best of anything evaluated: ` +
      `PR-AUC ${fmt(lgbm.minute.pr_auc)} against the threshold's ${fmt(threshold.minute.pr_auc)}.`,
    found:
      "On the ranking metric, the hypothesis looked confirmed. This is the point at which a " +
      "less careful project would have published.",
    href: "/models/lightgbm/",
    hrefLabel: "LightGBM model card",
  },
  {
    n: "06",
    title: "Unexpected results",
    expected: "Better ranking would mean better detection.",
    body:
      `It did not. LightGBM detected ${lgbm.event.events_detected} of ` +
      `${lgbm.event.n_events} events against the threshold's ${threshold.event.events_detected} ` +
      `— no more flares — and the confidence intervals on ROC-AUC overlapped the baseline's, ` +
      `so no model was statistically distinguishable from it.`,
    found:
      "Ranking and detection came apart. At a base rate near one percent, being better at " +
      "ordering minutes does not translate into catching more events, because the events were " +
      "already at the top of both orderings.",
    href: "/validation/#ranking",
    hrefLabel: "ROC and PR curves",
  },
  {
    n: "07",
    title: "False alarm analysis",
    expected: null,
    body:
      `Alarms were then counted as runs — contiguous blocks of predicted-positive minutes — ` +
      `because that is what an operator responds to. LightGBM raised ` +
      `${lgbm.event.false_event_runs.toLocaleString("en-US")} false runs against the ` +
      `threshold's ${threshold.event.false_event_runs}: about ` +
      `${alarmRatio.toFixed(0)} times as many, for no additional events detected.`,
    found:
      "This settled the question. The comparison is decided on the metric that matters " +
      "operationally, and on that metric the learned models lose decisively.",
    href: "/validation/#tradeoff",
    hrefLabel: "The recall–burden trade-off",
  },
  {
    n: "08",
    title: "Calibration",
    expected: null,
    body:
      `The remaining defence of a learned model is that its probabilities are useful even if ` +
      `its alarms are not. LightGBM's reliability diagram and Brier score (${fmt(m.calibration.brier, 4)}) ` +
      `are published so that argument can be evaluated rather than asserted.`,
    found:
      "The threshold detector emits a physical rate, not a probability, so it has no " +
      "reliability diagram at all. Where a comparison is impossible, no comparison is drawn.",
    href: "/validation/#calibration",
    hrefLabel: "The reliability diagram",
  },
  {
    n: "09",
    title: "Final scientific conclusion",
    expected: null,
    body:
      `A one-line detector on the SoLEXS count rate remains the operational recommendation. ` +
      `Machine learning ranks individual minutes better, detects no more flares, and raises ` +
      `roughly ${alarmRatio.toFixed(0)}× the false alarms. Added spectral resolution changed ` +
      `ROC-AUC by ${bench.ablation.delta_bands!.toFixed(4)} — a confirmed null.`,
    found:
      "The negative result is published at full weight, and the model that lost has the most " +
      "detailed card on the site. A platform whose central claim is that its evidence is " +
      "checkable earns that claim by publishing the finding a positive-result incentive would bury.",
    href: "/findings/",
    hrefLabel: "The findings",
  },
] as const;

// `produces` is omitted rather than set to undefined: the project builds under
// exactOptionalPropertyTypes, where an explicit undefined is not the same as an absent key.
const SPINE = STAGES.map((s) => ({
  n: s.n,
  title: s.title,
  ...(s.expected ? { produces: "expectation revised" } : {}),
  emphasis: s.n === "06" || s.n === "07",
  href: `#stage-${s.n}`,
}));
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Research journey — AdityaNet"
  description="How AdityaNet reached its conclusions: the research question, the hypothesis that failed, the baseline that won, the false alarm analysis that settled it."
>
  <AmbientBackdrop
    src="/video/space-galaxies.mp4"
    poster="/video/space-galaxies-poster.jpg"
    shape="frame"
    align="left"
  />

  <main id="main" class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 pb-32">
    <div class="section-tight flow">
      <PageHeader
        eyebrow="Orientation · research journey"
        title="The investigation, not just the answer"
        lede="Nine stages, in the order they happened — including the two where the result was not what this project expected, and the point at which a less careful version of it would have published."
      />
    </div>

    <PageContract
      what="A chronological account of how the flare-detection question was asked, tested, and answered — with the hypothesis that failed left in."
      trust="Every figure in this account is interpolated at build time from the same committed artifacts the evidence pages render from, so the story cannot keep repeating a number the benchmark no longer contains."
      verify={[
        { href: "/validation/", label: "The evidence for each stage", note: "Curves, calibration, error analysis" },
        { href: "/evidence/", label: "Trace any claim here", note: "Claim → artifact → commit" },
        { href: "/models/", label: "Every model that was tried", note: "Eight cards" },
        { href: "/data/card/#limitations", label: "What the data cannot support", note: "L-1 … L-10" },
      ]}
    />

    <Section id="spine" eyebrow="The shape" title="Nine stages">
      <div class="mt-2">
        <FlowDiagram
          stages={SPINE}
          direction="column"
          caption="Stages 6 and 7 are where the argument turns. Everything before them is a hypothesis being tested; everything after is a conclusion being constrained."
        />
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="stages" eyebrow="The account" title="What happened, in order" major ruled>
      <ol class="mt-2 space-y-4">
        {
          STAGES.map((s) => (
            <li id={`stage-${s.n}`} class="glass p-6 scroll-mt-24">
              <div class="flex items-baseline gap-4">
                <p class="font-mono text-2xs text-fg-subtle">{s.n}</p>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-fg">{s.title}</h3>
              </div>

              {s.expected && (
                <p class="mt-5 max-w-measure border-l border-line-subtle pl-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-subtle">
                  <span class="font-mono text-2xs tracking-[0.16em] uppercase">Expected · </span>
                  {s.expected}
                </p>
              )}

              <p class="mt-5 max-w-measure text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">{s.body}</p>
              <p class="mt-4 max-w-measure text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg">{s.found}</p>

              <p class="mt-5">
                <a href={s.href} class="link font-mono text-2xs">{s.hrefLabel} →</a>
              </p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </Section>

    <Section id="honest" eyebrow="One more thing" title="What this account leaves in" ruled>
      <div class="max-w-measure space-y-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        <p>
          The hypothesis in stage 2 was wrong, and it is left in the record rather than
          rewritten to match the outcome. So is stage 5, where the ranking metric made the
          hypothesis look confirmed — that is a real moment in the investigation, and removing it
          would make the eventual conclusion look more obvious than it was.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same discipline applies elsewhere on this platform. Six times the implementation
          contradicted the written specification; all six adjudications are published, including
          the amendment that was declined and the one that remains open. A
          <a href="/reproducibility/#lockfile" class="link"> digest mismatch</a> in the
          environment record was traced rather than silenced, and the trace is published as a
          case study.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of that is decoration. A platform that only ever shows its successes gives a
          reader no way to calibrate how much to believe it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="next" ruled>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <LearnMore href="/findings/" label="The conclusion" note="Stated at full weight" />
        <LearnMore href="/engineering/provenance/" label="Six contradictions" note="Each one adjudicated in public" />
        <LearnMore href="/start/" label="Where to go next" note="Five routes, one per visitor" />
      </div>
    </Section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
